; exit 77 (see .exit)
;
; A trap inside a `parallel` binding is the PROGRAM's trap, under either
; lowering.
;
; Under threads that is already so: the runtime's traps exit the whole
; process (`targetExitNum` is `exit_group` on Linux, and its comment
; records why that mattered before there was a second thread). Under
; processes the child dies alone, so `__axiom_par_join_proc` reads its
; wait status and exits with the same number - the child wrote the
; message. Status 77 is `vecGet`'s out-of-range trap
; (`docs/generics-design.md` section 4); this case pins it out of both
; lowerings, with `before` on stdout and nothing after the form.
;
; The join of `a` is what propagates, and `b` - which would have
; answered - is never reached: the body never runs, and nothing after
; the form does either.
(import IO)

(import Str)

(import Vec)

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  (let ((v vecNew))
    {
      (vecPush v 1)
      (println "before")
      (parallel p ((a (vecGet v 5)) (b 2))
        (println (+ a b)))
      ; Nothing after the form runs: the join of `a` re-raises 77. The
      ; tail is a CALL and not the literal 0 for `464-index-trap.ax`'s
      ; reason - `verify-stdlib-out.py` models a case's exit status from
      ; `main`'s tail when that tail is a literal, and this program's
      ; status comes from the child's trap. It is also the tripwire for
      ; the join swallowing the status: if it ever answers instead of
      ; exiting 77, this line runs and the golden says so.
      (printlnLit (strCStr "UNREACHABLE: the join returned"))
    }
  )
)
